    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>How Daylight Saving Time Is Handled</h2>
      <p>A time zone is not a fixed offset. Places change their clocks for daylight saving time, and governments occasionally change the rules altogether. This converter does not store its own table of offsets. It asks your browser, through the standard <code>Intl.DateTimeFormat</code> API, what the local time is in each IANA zone at the exact instant you picked. Browsers ship the IANA time zone database and update it with the browser, so the rules match the ones your operating system uses.</p>
      <ul class="content-list">
        <li><strong>United States and Canada:</strong> clocks go forward on the second Sunday in March and back on the first Sunday in November, at 02:00 local time.</li>
        <li><strong>UK and European Union:</strong> clocks change at 01:00 UTC on the last Sunday in March and the last Sunday in October.</li>
        <li><strong>Southern hemisphere:</strong> Australia (in the states that use DST), New Zealand and Chile move their clocks in the opposite season, so a gap to Europe can change by two hours over the year.</li>
        <li><strong>No DST:</strong> Pakistan, India, China, Japan, the UAE, Saudi Arabia and most of Africa keep the same offset all year.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Because of these different dates, the gap between two cities can change several times a year. New York and London are usually 5 hours apart, but for about three weeks in March and one week in late October/early November they are 4 hours apart.</p>

      <h2>Tips for Scheduling Across Time Zones</h2>
      <ul class="content-list">
        <li>Write meeting times with a city or an offset, such as "15:00 London (UTC+1)", not just "3 pm BST".</li>
        <li>Put the time in the organiser's zone in the calendar invite. Calendar apps convert it for each attendee using the same IANA data.</li>
        <li>Check the date as well as the time. A call at 17:00 in San Francisco is already the next morning in Asia.</li>
        <li>Rotate inconvenient slots when a team has no shared working hours, so the same people are not always on early or late calls.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
